# Who may reach mcpd. A template, not part of the manifests beside it.
#
# The extension is .example on purpose. `kubectl apply -f deploy/k8s/` applies
# every manifest in this directory, and a NetworkPolicy that names a namespace
# you do not have cuts mcpd off from everything with no error to read. Edit the
# selectors below, save it as networkpolicy.yaml, and apply that file by name.
#
# Read this before you rely on it, because the honest answer is narrower than
# the question usually asked. "Only these namespaces may reach ingest" is not
# something a NetworkPolicy can say here. mcpd serves OTLP ingest, the query
# API, the operator interface and /metrics on ONE port, and a policy selects
# pods and ports, never paths. So this gates the whole daemon: whatever you let
# in to write traces can also call /v1/api and read stored prompts and
# completions. The read key is what holds those two apart, not this file.
#
# What it does buy is still worth having. With no policy in the namespace, every
# pod in the cluster can open a connection to mcpd. With this, only the sources
# named below can.
#
# One trap before you apply it: some CNI plugins enforce policy on traffic
# arriving from the node, and the kubelet's liveness and readiness probes arrive
# that way. On those clusters, add the node CIDR under ipBlock or the pod never
# becomes ready and the rollout stalls.
#
# There is no egress rule here, so egress stays unrestricted. Adding one without
# meaning to breaks four things quietly, because mcpd logs a warning and carries
# on: alert webhooks, forwarding to another OTLP endpoint, the judge's model
# endpoint, and an external Postgres or ClickHouse.
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: mcpd
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/name: mcpd
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app.kubernetes.io/name: mcpd
  # Ingress only. Naming Egress here with no egress rule below would deny every
  # outbound connection, including DNS.
  policyTypes:
    - Ingress
  ingress:
    - from:
        # A whole namespace. Kubernetes sets kubernetes.io/metadata.name on
        # every namespace itself, so this needs no labelling first.
        - namespaceSelector:
            matchLabels:
              kubernetes.io/metadata.name: REPLACE_WITH_YOUR_AGENT_NAMESPACE
        # Pods in mcpd's own namespace. A podSelector with no namespaceSelector
        # beside it never reaches across a namespace boundary.
        - podSelector:
            matchLabels:
              app.kubernetes.io/part-of: REPLACE_WITH_YOUR_AGENT_FLEET
      ports:
        # The container port, not a Service port. A policy is enforced at the
        # pod, so a Service in front of it makes no difference here. This has to
        # match containerPort in deployment.yaml.
        - protocol: TCP
          port: 4317
